As a Premier League club involved in [[UEFA club competitions|UEFA competitions]], Arsenal entered in the third round and were drawn away to [[EFL League One]] club [[Port Vale F.C.|Port Vale]]. At [[Vale Park]], on 24 September 2025, Arsenal won 2–0 with goals from [[Eberechi Eze]] – his first goal for the club – and [[Leandro Trossard]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=24 September 2025 |first=Gary |last=Rose|title=Port Vale 0-2 Arsenal: Eberechi Eze score first Gunners goal in EFL Cup win |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/live/c5ygpez5znyt |access-date=4 February 2026 |website=BBC Sport}}</ref> In the fourth round Arsenal were drawn at home to fellow [[Premier League]] club [[Brighton & Hove Albion F.C.|Brighton & Hove Albion]]. At the [[Emirates Stadium]] on 29 October 2025, Arsenal won 2–0 again with goals for the Gunners from [[Ethan Nwaneri]] and [[Bukayo Saka]] which sent the Gunners through to the quarter-finals.<ref>{{Cite web |date=29 October 2025 |title=Arsenal 2-0 Brighton: Ethan Nwaneri and Bukayo Saka goals send Arsenal through |url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/live/cd9k0xe2nlkt |first=Alex |last=Howell |access-date=4 February 2026 |website=BBC Sport}}</ref> In the quarter-finals Arsenal were drawn against another Premier League club in [[Crystal Palace F.C.|Crystal Palace]]. At the Emirates Stadium on 23 December 2025, Arsenal drew 1–1 with an [[own goal]] from [[Maxence Lacroix]] being equalised by [[Marc Guéhi]]. As the game finished level after regulation time, it went straight to a [[Penalty shoot-out (association football)|penalty shoot-out]] which Arsenal won 8–7 with [[Martin Ødegaard]], [[Declan Rice]], Saka, Trossard, [[Mikel Merino]], [[Riccardo Calafiori]], [[Jurriën Timber]] and [[William Saliba]] all scoring their spot-kicks for the Gunners and [[Jean-Philippe Mateta]], [[Justin Devenny]], [[Will Hughes]], [[Borna Sosa]], [[Jefferson Lerma]], [[Adam Wharton]] and [[Christantus Uche]] all converted their penalty kicks; Lacroix missed the decisive penalty kick to send Arsenal through to the semi-finals.<ref>{{Cite web |date=23 December 2025 |first=Alex |last=Howell |title=Arsenal beat Crystal Palace on penalties for place in EFL Cup semi-final against Chelsea|url=https://www.bbc.co.uk/sport/football/live/c0582m4n108t |access-date=4 February 2026 |website=BBC Sport}}</ref> In the semi-finals Arsenal were drawn against another fellow [[Premier League]] club and [[Arsenal F.C.–Chelsea F.C. rivalry|rivals]] [[Chelsea F.C.|Chelsea]] over two legs. Arsenal won the first leg 3–2 at [[Stamford Bridge (stadium)|Stamford Bridge]] on 14 January 2026 with two goals for Chelsea from [[Alejandro Garnacho]] and goals for Arsenal from [[Ben White (footballer)|Ben White]], [[Viktor Gyökeres]] and [[Martín Zubimendi]].<ref>{{Cite web |date=14 January 2026 |title=Chelsea 2-3 Arsenal: Viktor Gyokeres ends goal drought as Gunners edge first leg of Carabao Cup semi-final |url=https://www.skysports.com/football/news/11095/13493486/chelsea-2-3-arsenal-viktor-gyokeres-ends-goal-drought-as-gunners-edge-first-leg-of-carabao-cup-semi-final |access-date=4 February 2026 |website=Sky Sports}}</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=14 January 2026 |title=Chelsea 2-3 Arsenal: Gunners have EFL Cup semi-final advantage but Garnacho gives Blues hope|first=Nizaar |last=Kinsella |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/live/cp82v1qp9e1t |access-date=5 February 2026 |website=BBC Sport}}</ref> Arsenal won the second leg 1–0 at the Emirates Stadium on 3 February 2026 with a goal from former Chelsea player [[Kai Havertz]] to give Arsenal a 4–2 aggregate victory and send them through to the final.<ref>{{Cite web |date=3 February 2026 |title=Carabao Cup: Arsenal beat Chelsea to reach first major cup final for almost six years|first=Alex |last=Howell |url=https://www.bbc.com/sport/football/live/c07xyy0j4l5t|access-date=4 February 2026 |website=BBC Sport}}</ref>

