As a Premier League club involved in UEFA competitions, Manchester City entered in the third round and were drawn away to [[EFL League One]] club [[Huddersfield Town A.F.C.|Huddersfield Town]]. At [[Kirklees Stadium]], on 24 September 2025, Manchester City won 2–0 with goals from [[Phil Foden]] and [[Savinho]].<ref>{{cite web |title=Foden, Savinho score as Man City ease past Huddersfield |url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/report/_/gameId/758198 |agency=[[PA Media]]|website=ESPN|date=24 September 2025 |access-date=4 February 2026}}</ref> In the fourth round, they were drawn away to [[EFL Championship]] side [[Swansea City A.F.C.|Swansea City]], played away at [[Swansea.com Stadium]] on 29 October 2025. The match finished 3–1 for the visitors, with [[Gonçalo Franco]] opening the scoring for Swansea City before three goals from [[Jérémy Doku]], [[Omar Marmoush]] and [[Rayan Cherki]] for Manchester City.<ref>{{cite web |title=Erling Haaland sits as Man City reach Carabao Cup quarters |agency=PA Media |url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/report/_/gameId/759243 |access-date=4 February 2026|website=ESPN |date=29 October 2025}}</ref> In the quarter-finals, City were drawn at home to fellow Premier League side [[Brentford F.C.|Brentford]], where they won 2–0 at [[City of Manchester Stadium]] on 17 December 2025 with goals from Cherki and Savinho.<ref>{{cite web |title=Cherki channels Haaland in leading Man City to Carabao Cup semifinals|url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/report/_/gameId/760343 |agency=[[Associated Press]] |website=ESPN|access-date=4 February 2026|date=17 December 2025}}</ref> In the two-legged semi-final, City were drawn against [[2025 EFL Cup final|cup holders]] [[Newcastle United F.C.|Newcastle United]], with the first leg played at [[St James' Park]] on 13 January 2026. City won 2–0 courtesy of freshly-signed [[Antoine Semenyo]] and Cherki.<ref>{{cite web |title=Semenyo scores again as Man City get semifinal edge over Newcastle|url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/report/_/gameId/401842433 |agency=PA Media |website=ESPN|access-date=4 February 2026 |date=13 January 2026}}</ref> The second leg was played on 4 February, with Manchester City winning 3–1. Marmoush scored twice and [[Tijjani Reijnders]] scored once before [[Anthony Elanga]] picked up a consolation goal for Newcastle, giving Manchester City a 5–1 aggregate victory and sending them to the final.<ref>{{cite web |title=Manchester City roll past Newcastle into cup final with Arsenal|url=https://www.espn.com/soccer/report/_/gameId/401842434|website=ESPN|agency=PA Media |access-date=4 February 2026 |date=4 February 2026}}</ref>
